{
  "title": "Lean-STaR: Learning to Interleave Thinking and Proving",
  "authors": [
    "Haohan Lin",
    "Zhiqing Sun",
    "Sean Welleck",
    "Yiming Yang"
  ],
  "year": 2025,
  "arxivId": "2407.10040",
  "abstract": "Lean-STaR is a framework for training language models to produce informal thoughts prior to each step of a formal proof, boosting the model's theorem-proving capabilities. By generating synthetic thoughts and fine-tuning the model with expert iteration, Lean-STaR significantly outperforms base models, achieving a pass rate of 36.1% on the miniF2F-test benchmark.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "The core idea of Lean-STaR is to **interleave thinking and proving** by generating informal thoughts prior to each step of a formal proof. This approach aims to leverage the wealth of informal information that is not present in formal proofs, such as the underlying thought process prior to each step. By **augmenting formal proofs with natural language thoughts**, Lean-STaR enables language models to better understand the reasoning behind each step, leading to improved theorem-proving capabilities.",
